// The standard interface, implemented against `mizu.doir`.
//
// `standard.doir` declares the interface; this file is the half that says what
// each name *is*. Compile it with `test_standard_mizu.doir`, which
// early_includes `mizu.doir` ahead of it - almost every name below is one of
// that file's.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What a line in this file is
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Most of them are renamings: a name from `standard.doir` bound to a
// `mizu.doir` primitive of the same arity. The reflection half is nothing but
// renamings and could not be anything else, which is what discharges R-Square
// below - the checked and unchecked halves of the interface are one
// instruction under two names rather than two paths to keep in agreement.
//
// The rest have bodies, which they can have because `compiler.return` is
// nameable from here. A DOIR function returns by saying which register it
// hands back (WF-Term), and that - not arithmetic, not emission - is the only
// thing a body ever needed the assembler layer for. Three kinds appear:
//
//   - control flow (`if`, `while`, `for`): an inlined function that emits
//     branches around blocks the caller quoted, which `execute` splices in
//     where they stand;
//   - the dispatch (`add` and everything after it): an inlined function that
//     asks `T` which of Mizu's three readings of a 64 bit register it wants,
//     and emits the matching instruction;
//   - nothing, still. What is left as a TODO below is what Mizu has no
//     instruction for, or what no instruction can reach the store with - not
//     what the layering forbids.
//
// That last line is the finding, and it has moved: the file used to record
// that `standard.doir` could not be implemented against `mizu.doir` at all
// without a way to return. It can, and the holes that remain are the backend's
// rather than the language's.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The `compiler.*` names this file uses
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `compiler.return` and `compiler.yield`, which say which register a body
// hands back (WF-Term, see `return` below); `compiler.assembler.pin_register`,
// which is how two arms of a construct agree on where their result lands;
// `compiler.base_type`, the one type constructor whose result can be asked
// about afterwards (see `f64`); and `compiler.byte_pointer`, the type of a
// string literal. Every one of them is a construct of the language rather than
// of the backend - `compiler.override_fallback_schedule` at the bottom is the
// exception that proves it, and is the compiler being told about the backend
// rather than the other way round. Everything else below is `mizu.doir`'s.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The semantics this file is written against
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// See `standard.doir`'s own header, which this one does not repeat. The rule
// names (M-Flag, M-Ctor, R-Qual, ...) are `doir.spec.typ`'s.

std_mizu_include_path_builtin : compiler.byte_pointer = "mizu.doir"
_ : _ = early_include(std_mizu_include_path_builtin)


std : namespace = {
	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// The assembler layer's types
	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// `standard.doir` assumes `u1`, `u8` and `pointer_sized` are given to it. All
	// three are `mizu.u64`, because a Mizu register *is* 64 bits and there is
	// nothing narrower for them to be: every instruction reads and writes whole
	// registers, and the only narrowing in the machine is what a `stack_store_u8`
	// writes to memory.
	//
	// Aliases rather than `type_base` types of the widths their names claim.
	// Declaring a real 8 bit type is possible - `mizu.doir.type_base` does it - but
	// it buys a name for something the backend cannot represent, and S-Struct then
	// (correctly) refuses to pass it to arithmetic that wants 64 bits, with no
	// widening conversion anywhere to get it back. A backend with narrower
	// registers would declare them properly here; this one has one width.
	u1 : alias = mizu.u64
	u8 : alias = mizu.u64
	pointer_sized : alias = mizu.u64

	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// The assembler layer's numeric kinds
	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Mizu has one register width and three readings of it - unsigned, two's
	// complement signed, and IEEE double - with a different instruction per
	// reading and nothing in a `mizu.u64` that says which one a program meant. So
	// `std.add` and its neighbours cannot be renamings the way the reflection
	// half of this file is: each has to ask its type which reading it wants and
	// emit the matching instruction.
	//
	// `attribute_id` is where the answer is kept (M-Attr): it is the one number a
	// type already carries, so the three kinds are a single tag rather than two
	// bits, and unsigned is the *absence* of one - `type_attribute_id` answers
	// zero for a type that was never pinned to a slot. The ids are interned names
	// rather than literals, which is what `intern_name` is for; a program that
	// pins its own types to slots draws from the same space and will not collide.
	unsigned_kind : pointer_sized = 0
	signed_name : compiler.byte_pointer = "signed"
	signed_kind : pointer_sized = mizu.doir.intern_name(signed_name)
	float_name : compiler.byte_pointer = "float"
	float_kind : pointer_sized = mizu.doir.intern_name(float_name)

	// `type_base` rather than `alias`, and no `unique`. The tag has to land on an
	// entity of its own - `set_attribute_id` acts through aliases (P3), so tagging
	// a name for `mizu.u64` would tag `mizu.u64`, and every unsigned type with it.
	// A fresh `type_base` of the same layout is a distinct entity without being a
	// distinct type: S-Struct compares <size, alignment, unique>, so `f64` still
	// agrees with `mizu.u64` and can be handed to the instructions below.
	//
	// `unique` is what the names deserve and what a real backend would write, and
	// it is exactly what this file cannot afford - it is the `u8`/`u1` argument
	// above again. A unique `f64` stops agreeing with `mizu.u64`, and every
	// instruction in `mizu.doir` takes `mizu.u64`, so the type would have nothing
	// to be passed to. The cost of not doing it is that S-Struct lets an `f64` and
	// a `u64` convert silently; nothing performs that conversion today either
	// (see `<divergences>`), so today the two costs cancel.
	//
	// `compiler.base_type` rather than `mizu.doir.type_base`, which is the same
	// constructor on the VM and is what the note above `u1` points at. The VM one
	// cannot be used *and then asked about*: every comptime evaluation writes its
	// result back as a `ComptimeNumber` on the call, `type_base`'s result is the
	// call's own entity id, and the next `canon.sort` renumbers every entity in
	// the module - so the type is left holding the id it used to have. The
	// argument loader reads a `ComptimeNumber` in preference to the live entity,
	// so `attributes.get_id_of_type(f64)` then asks about whatever now lives at
	// that id. A `compiler.base_type` is folded by `opt.foldBaseTypes` instead,
	// which leaves a type and no number.
	%64 : pointer_sized = 64
	i64 : type = compiler.base_type(%64, %64)
	i64_pointer : type = types.pointer(i64)
	_ : type = mizu.doir.type_set_attribute_id(i64, signed_kind)
	f64 : type = compiler.base_type(%64, %64)
	f64_pointer : type = types.pointer(f64)
	_ : type = mizu.doir.type_set_attribute_id(f64, float_kind)
	u64 : alias = mizu.u64
	u64_pointer : type = types.pointer(u64)

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Operations on types
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	types : namespace = {
		// A modifier edits the type it is handed and yields an alias to it
		// (M-Flag); a constructor allocates a new one and leaves its argument
		// untouched (M-Ctor). One signature, because only the instruction knows
		// which of the two it is doing.
		export modifier_function : alias = mizu.type_modifier_t
		export constructor_function : alias = mizu.type_modifier_t

		export set_attribute_id : alias = mizu.doir.type_set_attribute_id
		export array : alias = mizu.doir.type_array

		export comptime : alias = mizu.doir.type_comptime
		export never_monomorphize : alias = mizu.doir.type_never_monomorphize
		export pointer : alias = mizu.doir.type_pointer
		export union : alias = mizu.doir.type_union

		// M-Unique. The one modifier that does not act through aliases, which
		// is the instruction's business rather than anything visible here.
		export unique : alias = mizu.doir.type_make_unique

		export size_function : alias = mizu.type_query_t
		export size_bits : alias = mizu.doir.type_size_bits
		export align_bits : alias = mizu.doir.type_align_bits

		// Asks a field rather than a type, so its own signature: where the
		// field begins inside the aggregate that declares it, in bits.
		export field_function : alias = mizu.field_query_t
		export field_offset_bits : alias = mizu.doir.field_offset_bits
		_ : type = comptime(field_function)

		// Every query sharing this signature asks a question *about a type*,
		// and a type is compile time known (C-Type), so every answer is. Saying
		// so is not decoration: `sema.bubbleComptime` runs once, in
		// `canonicalizeSchedule`, and by then the query it would have marked is
		// still inside a function body where nothing can evaluate it - so
		// without the flag on the signature, `opt.inlineFunctions` reaches the
		// call at the site that instantiated it first and replaces it with the
		// *bytes of the instruction*. The arithmetic below reads these answers
		// at compile time to choose what to emit, and an emitted
		// `type_attribute_id` is not an answer.
		//
		// Through the alias, onto `mizu.type_query_t` itself (P3), which is
		// what every other query in this file is declared with.
		_ : type = comptime(size_function)

		// TODO: `pack` has nothing to set. Every other modifier here is one bit
		// of `doir.interface_.Flags`, and there is no `Pack` bit - the layout
		// modifiers were never implemented, so C-Order (layout modifiers do not
		// commute, lexical order decides) has nothing to order yet.
		// export pack : alias = ...

		// The one member here that emits rather than answers: it takes an
		// address, so the offset folds at compile time and the add is code.
		// It lives in `types` because that is where the field it reads comes
		// from, not because it is a type query.
		//
		// `field` is the field *declaration* a dotted `vec2.x` resolves to
		// (R-Qual descends into an aggregate the way it does a namespace), not
		// a value - the only place in this file that takes one. It is declared
		// `comptime.u64` for the same reason `mizu.doir`'s encoders declare
		// their immediates that way: inside the body it is a parameter, and a
		// parameter is only compile time known if its type says so.
		//
		// `field_offset_bits` answers bits and addresses count bytes, so the
		// `shift_right` is the divide - and both fold, because a field's
		// position is compile time known (C-Type). Neither folds *here*:
		// `computeCompilerNamespace` declines to fold inside a function body,
		// so `always_inline` is what gets them to a call site where they can.
		field_pointer_t : type = (Tobj : deduced type, object : Tobj, field : mizu.comptime.u64) -> byte_pointer
		_ : type = functions.always_inline(field_pointer_t)
		// Emits; never runs. Without this a call on a compile time known object
		// would be handed to the evaluator, which has no instruction for it.
		_ : type = functions.never_comptime(field_pointer_t)
		export field_pointer : field_pointer_t = {
			%3 : index = 3
			offset_bits : index = field_offset_bits(field)
			offset : index = compiler.shift_right(offset_bits, %3)
			ptr : _ = mizu.add(object, offset)
			_ : _ = return(ptr)
		}

		// Placeholder, as in `standard.doir`: T-Self has no resolution rule.
		export self : alias = u8
	}

	export byte : alias = u8
	export byte_pointer : type = types.pointer(byte)
	export null_terminated_byte_pointer : alias = byte_pointer
	export index : alias = pointer_sized

	// I-Import. `early_include` is a builtin of the language rather than of the
	// compiler namespace, so it can be renamed from here.
	export import : alias = early_include

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Reflection: the entity store, exposed to the program
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	meta : namespace = {
		export entity : alias = pointer_sized

		// The two crossings. `reflect` is an identity on the register - a
		// comptime argument of type `type` reaches the VM as its entity id
		// already - and exists so that crossing is a call the program makes.
		export reflect : alias = mizu.doir.reflect
		export unreflect_alias : alias = mizu.doir.unreflect_alias

		// TODO: `unreflect` needs to splice an entity's *value* into the call
		// that asked for it, the way `mizu.doir.execute` splices a block. No
		// instruction does that yet.
		// export unreflect : alias = ...

		export rename : alias = mizu.doir.entity_rename

		// The three readings of a call site (R-Args). Compile time known for
		// `types.size_bits`' reason, stated on the signature for exactly its
		// reason too: these are the loop control of the unroll in
		// `std.functions.impl.pass_arguments`, and `opt.inlineFunctions`
		// reaches a call to one before the late evaluation round the schedule
		// adds for that unroll - so without the flag the call is replaced by
		// the *bytes of the instruction* and the program executes a reflection
		// opcode. An emitted `argument_count` is not an answer.
		//
		// Unlike the type queries, what makes the answer known is the argument
		// rather than C-Type: an entity id is comptime only if whoever supplied
		// it made it so, which is why `opt.lowerFunctionCalls` hands these a
		// `Number` holding the id and not the entity.
		export entity_function : alias = mizu.entity_query_t
		export entity_with_number_function : alias = mizu.entity_with_number_t
		// `types.comptime`, not a bare `comptime`: these sit directly in
		// `meta`, where the type namespace's members are a `types.` away.
		_ : type = types.comptime(entity_function)
		_ : type = types.comptime(entity_with_number_function)

		export argument_count : alias = mizu.doir.argument_count
		export argument : alias = mizu.doir.argument
		export parameter : alias = mizu.doir.parameter

		// R-Square, discharged by construction: `meta.types.M(reflect(T))` and
		// `reflect(std.types.M(T))` are not two paths that have to be kept in
		// agreement here, they are the same instruction under two names. The
		// unchecked half of the interface collapses into the checked one
		// because neither can be a function with a body.
		types : namespace = {
			export is : alias = mizu.doir.type_is
			export set_attribute_id : alias = mizu.doir.type_set_attribute_id
			export modifier_function : alias = mizu.type_modifier_t
			export constructor_function : alias = mizu.type_modifier_t
			export array : alias = mizu.doir.type_array
			export comptime : alias = mizu.doir.type_comptime
			export pointer : alias = mizu.doir.type_pointer
			export union : alias = mizu.doir.type_union
			export unique : alias = mizu.doir.type_make_unique
			export size_bits : alias = mizu.doir.type_size_bits
			export align_bits : alias = mizu.doir.type_align_bits
		}

		// Seven queries rather than a `location` aggregate with a `get` that
		// returns it: a returned struct needs a function body (see the header),
		// so each field is asked for separately, of the entity directly.
		source_location : namespace = {
			export get_file : alias = mizu.doir.source_location_file
			export get_start_byte : alias = mizu.doir.source_location_start_byte
			export get_end_byte : alias = mizu.doir.source_location_end_byte
			export get_start_line : alias = mizu.doir.source_location_start_line
			export get_start_column : alias = mizu.doir.source_location_start_column
			export get_end_line : alias = mizu.doir.source_location_end_line
			export get_end_column : alias = mizu.doir.source_location_end_column
		}
	}

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Operations on functions
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	functions : namespace = {
		export always_inline : alias = mizu.doir.type_always_inline
		export always_flatten : alias = mizu.doir.type_always_flatten

		// The one flag that says a function *cannot* be folded. A construct that
		// emits code rather than computing a value needs it: its arguments are
		// usually compile time known, and without this the evaluator runs it on
		// the VM instead of letting it emit.
		export never_comptime : alias = mizu.doir.type_no_comptime

		// TODO: `abi_rename` is `(T: deduced type, function: T, new_name: ...)`
		// - three parameters, where an `Opcode` has two operands and a result.
		// `meta.rename` is the two-parameter form it would be built out of.

		// TODO: `add_overload` is blocked upstream of the instruction set:
		// `add_overload_t` returns `_`, so there is no type for an overload set
		// and nothing for D-Deduce to solve. See `<divergences>`.

		// -------------------------------------------------------------------
		// The calling convention
		// -------------------------------------------------------------------
		// Four jumps between two labels, which is all a call is once the
		// labels exist. `opt.claimFunctionLabels` puts an entry and an exit
		// label into every function that still has to be emitted and records
		// the pair on the function; `opt.lowerFunctionCalls` then hands `call`
		// the entry label and `return` the exit one. So nothing below has to
		// *find* a label - it is given one, and `find_label` turns it into the
		// address the jump takes. That is the whole of the function-to-label
		// problem, and it is solved in the store rather than here.
		//
		// `ra` is Mizu's return address register (x21, RISC-V's layout - see
		// `mizu.opcode`'s `Registers`). `jump_to` hands back a pointer to the
		// instruction after itself, so a call gets its return address for free
		// and only has to say which register it lands in.
		//
		// All four are `always_inline` and `never_comptime`: they emit, their
		// one argument is a compile time known entity, and without the second
		// flag `sema.bubbleComptime` marks them folded and the evaluator runs
		// the jumps instead of letting them be emitted - the same reason
		// `if` and `while` above carry it.
		impl : namespace = {
			frame_t : type = (target: meta.entity)
			_ : type = functions.always_inline(frame_t)
			_ : type = functions.never_comptime(frame_t)

			// One 8 byte slot, holding the return address `call` left in `ra`.
			// It has to be spilled rather than left there because a call the
			// body makes overwrites it, and the spill is unconditional because
			// nothing here knows yet whether the body makes one - a leaf
			// function pays a push and a pop it did not need. See
			// `<divergences>`.
			export prologue : frame_t = {
				zero : mizu.u64 = 0
				_ : _ = compiler.assembler.pin_register(mizu.u64, zero, mizu.x0)

				%8 : pointer_sized = 8
				_ : _ = mizu.stack_push_immediate(%8)

				// Named, not computed: `ra` already holds the return address
				// on entry, so the pin is the whole declaration - the trick
				// `if` uses to name `x0`.
				link : pointer_sized = 0
				_ : _ = compiler.assembler.pin_register(pointer_sized, link, mizu.ra)
				_ : _ = mizu.stack_store_u64(link, zero)
			}

			export epilogue : frame_t = {
				zero : mizu.u64 = 0
				_ : _ = compiler.assembler.pin_register(mizu.u64, zero, mizu.x0)

				link : pointer_sized = mizu.stack_load_u64(zero)
				%8 : pointer_sized = 8
				_ : _ = mizu.stack_pop_immediate(%8)
				_ : _ = mizu.jump_to(link)
			}

			// Where the whole convention is stated: `a0`. The machine's own
			// `argument_registers(a0, a7)` says where arguments go and the
			// compiler reads `slot` off it, but a result has no class to be
			// the first member of, so this file names the register. Naming one
			// is this file's business - `prologue` names `ra` and `if` names
			// `x0` - and it is the only place the result convention is
			// written down.
			result_register : alias = mizu.a0

			// One move per argument, and the loop that writes them is a
			// *comptime* one: each argument goes in a different register, so
			// there is nothing a runtime loop could emit once. The recursion
			// is the loop - `execute_if` splices the arm only when the
			// condition holds, so it terminates by not being spliced, which
			// is the same mechanism the dispatches at the bottom of this file
			// use to choose an arm.
			//
			// Loop control is written in raw `mizu.*` rather than in `add` and
			// `is_less` below, for the dispatches' reason and more sharply: a
			// `never_comptime` comparison would *emit* the test instead of
			// folding it, and an unroll whose index is not a number is not an
			// unroll.
			// `i` is `comptime`, and that is not decoration either. The loop
			// control has to fold in the *declaration*, because `deepCopy`
			// carries the comptime flags into every inlined copy and
			// `opt.inlineFunctions` reaches a call it cannot see is comptime
			// before the evaluator does - which is how the dispatches at the
			// bottom of this file manage it, with both operands of their
			// `mizu.set_if_*` known there. A plain parameter is not known, so
			// `set_if_less(i, count)` would be replaced by the bytes of the
			// instruction and the unroll would never run.
			pass_arguments_t : type = (function: meta.entity, call: meta.entity, i: mizu.comptime.u64)
			_ : type = functions.always_inline(pass_arguments_t)
			_ : type = functions.never_comptime(pass_arguments_t)

			export pass_arguments : pass_arguments_t = {
				count : index = meta.argument_count(call)
				more : u1 = mizu.set_if_less(i, count)
				arm : block = {
					// Both sides of the convention, named rather than
					// computed. `argument` hands back the argument's
					// declaration and `parameter` the declaration it binds
					// (WF-Arg makes both of those register names), and
					// `unreflect_alias` turns an entity back into a name for
					// it - so nothing is copied to get at either.
					//
					// `mizu.u64` and not `_`: D-Deduce solves parameters, not
					// return types, so a hole here has nothing to be solved
					// from. Every type in this layer is 64 bits wide.
					a : meta.entity = meta.argument(call, i)
					p : meta.entity = meta.parameter(function, i)
					value : mizu.u64 = meta.unreflect_alias(a)
					slot : mizu.u64 = meta.unreflect_alias(p)

					// The move, and then the only thing that says where it
					// went. `alias_temporaries` puts `moved` in the parameter's
					// register, and the parameter's register is already the
					// convention: `opt.assignTemporaries` numbers a parameter
					// `-(index + 1)` and `opt.mapTemporaries` reads the i'th
					// member of the machine's argument class off that. So no
					// register is named here, `a0` is not spelled, and the
					// caller and the callee cannot disagree about the slot
					// because they are reading one fact.
					//
					// `copy` rather than aliasing the argument itself: the
					// argument is the caller's value and may be live past the
					// call or passed to a second one in another position, and
					// a temporary united with two parameters would be asked
					// for two registers at once.
					moved : _ = std.copy(value)
					_ : _ = unsafe.alias_temporaries(mizu.u64, moved, slot)

					%1 : index = 1
					next : index = mizu.add(i, %1)
					_ : _ = pass_arguments(function, call, next)
				}
				_ : _ = execute_if(arm, more)
			}

			transfer_t : type = (target: meta.entity, function: meta.entity, call: meta.entity) -> mizu.u64
			_ : type = functions.always_inline(transfer_t)
			_ : type = functions.never_comptime(transfer_t)

			export call : transfer_t = {
				// The arguments first, and above the transfer: a move after
				// the jump is a move the callee never sees.
				%0 : index = 0
				_ : _ = pass_arguments(function, call, %0)

				// `target` is the callee's *entry label*, not the callee -
				// see the header above. `find_label` reads the id
				// `opt.mizu.materializeLabels` left on it.
				addr : _ = find_label(target)
				link : _ = jump_to(addr)
				_ : _ = compiler.assembler.pin_register(pointer_sized, link, mizu.ra)

				// Named, not computed, exactly as `prologue` names `ra`: the
				// callee has already written this register, so the pin is the
				// whole declaration and no load may be emitted for it. The
				// `copy` is what carries it into the call site's own register,
				// which `return` below pins it to - so the result is in the
				// register the rest of the block expects without the
				// allocator being told which one that is.
				returned : mizu.u64 = 0
				_ : _ = compiler.assembler.pin_register(mizu.u64, returned, result_register)
				// `std.` qualified: `return` here would find `impl.return`
				// below, since R-Unqual takes the nearest and the nearest is
				// the member declared beside this one.
				result : _ = copy(returned)
				_ : _ = std.return(result)
			}

			return_t : type = (T: deduced type, value: T, target: meta.entity)
			_ : type = functions.always_inline(return_t)
			_ : type = functions.never_comptime(return_t)

			export return : return_t = {
				// Above the jump, or the callee leaves through the teardown
				// before the result has been put anywhere the caller looks.
				returned : T = copy(value)
				_ : _ = compiler.assembler.pin_register(T, returned, result_register)

				// The exit label, so every `return` in a body shares one
				// teardown rather than repeating it (WF-Term).
				addr : _ = find_label(target)
				_ : _ = jump_to(addr)
			}
		}
	}

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Diagnostics
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Two parameters, not three: the message and the entity to point at have
	// already spent both of an `Opcode`'s operands, so `standard.doir`'s `help`
	// string has nowhere to go. The location is the entity itself rather than a
	// `location` value, for the reason `meta.source_location` above gives.
	unsafe : namespace = {
		export alias_temporaries : alias = compiler.assembler.alias_temporaries
	}

	diagnostics : namespace = {
		export info : alias = mizu.doir.diagnostic_info
		export warning : alias = mizu.doir.diagnostic_warning
		export error : alias = mizu.doir.diagnostic_error
	}

	attributes : namespace = {
		export get_id_of_type : alias = mizu.doir.type_attribute_id
		export get_id_of_name : alias = mizu.doir.intern_name

		// TODO: `get`, `add` and `get_or_add` attach a component chosen by a
		// runtime id. libECRS types its components at compile time, so there is
		// no store operation for them to be an instruction over yet.

		// TODO: `get_id` is the overload set of the two above, and waits on
		// `add_overload`.
	}

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Values
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// TODO: `cast` is a dispatch on the width of `Tout` across
	// `mizu.convert_to_u8` / `_u16` / `_u32` / `_u64` and the float converts,
	// which is a body. `mizu.convert_to_u64` is the identity case and the only
	// one nameable from here.
	export cast : alias = mizu.convert_to_u64

	// `copy` belongs here and is defined with the dispatches at the bottom
	// instead, for `transition_comptime_to_runtime`'s reason below: it shares
	// that one's type, which a dispatch declares.

	// TODO: `move` invalidates its argument (V-Move) and is otherwise `copy`.
	// Nothing enforces the invalidation yet - that needs liveness - so binding
	// it to the same instruction would be a claim this file cannot make.

	// `transition_comptime_to_runtime` belongs here and is defined with the
	// dispatches at the bottom instead: it is one, and by C-Order a dispatch
	// cannot be written above `execute_if`.

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Control flow
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// `execute` splices a quoted block into the call that ran it, at compile
	// time. Everything below is built out of it: a control flow construct is an
	// inlined function that emits the branches and lets `execute` put the
	// caller's blocks between them.
	export execute : alias = mizu.doir.execute
	export execute_if : alias = mizu.doir.execute_if

	// The one `compiler.*` name in this file, and it is a language construct
	// rather than a backend one: WF-Term's `return`, which says *which register*
	// a body hands back. Nothing below the language can spell it - an emitted
	// instruction can only write to the return register, which is no help to a
	// body with two exit points. `standard.doir` declares `return` and `yield`
	// with exactly this signature.
	export return : alias = compiler.return
	export yield : alias = compiler.yield

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// `if`
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// The shape every construct here takes: an inlined function whose body is
	// half object level and half meta level. `find_label`, `set_if_equal` and
	// `branch_to` are instructions the program will run; the two `execute`s are
	// the compiler's, and splice the caller's blocks in where they stand.
	//
	// That split is the reason this file nominates a schedule of its own. A
	// meta-level call reading a `block` *parameter* has nothing to read until a
	// call site binds one, so it can only run after `inlineFunctions` has
	// substituted the arguments - which is later than `mizu.doir`'s schedule
	// evaluates anything.
	//
	// Both arms name their result with `return`, which is what puts them in one
	// register: the union is untagged (nothing records which branch ran), so
	// either they agree or the result is discarded.
	if_t : type = (condition: u1, then: block, else: block) -> _
	_ : type = functions.always_inline(if_t)
	// Emits; never runs. A condition and two quoted blocks are all comptime as
	// often as not, and without this `sema.bubbleComptime` marks every call
	// compile time known and the evaluator assembles a program out of the very
	// branch instructions this exists to emit.
	_ : type = functions.never_comptime(if_t)
	export if : if_t = {
		// `x0` reads as zero on every Mizu machine, so the compare costs no
		// instruction - only the pin that says which register it is.
		zero : mizu.u64 = 0
		_ : _ = compiler.assembler.pin_register(mizu.u64, zero, mizu.x0)

		// Named before the `label()` that defines it (R-Order): a block's
		// declarations are mutually visible, which is what lets the branch
		// forward.
		else_addr : _ = find_label(else_label)
		end_addr : _ = find_label(end_label)
		// `mizu.set_if_equal` rather than `is_equal`, which is now the
		// dispatch below and would be a whole type query to answer a question
		// this already knows: `condition` is a `u1`.
		inverted : _ = mizu.set_if_equal(condition, zero)
		_ : _ = branch_to(inverted, else_addr)

		taken : _ = execute(then)
		_ : _ = jump_to(end_addr)

		else_label : _ = label()
		not_taken : _ = execute(else)
		// The two arms yield one value, so they have to land in one register -
		// whichever the allocator picks, as long as it is the same one. Two
		// pins to `mizu.x1` said that by naming a register, which is a
		// machine's business and not this function's: `x1` is a caller-saved
		// temporary here and would be something else on a backend with a
		// different file, and a hand-picked register is one the allocator
		// cannot reuse. `unsafe` because the allocator trusts it: a range it
		// is told to merge is one it will not reuse in between.
		_ : _ = unsafe.alias_temporaries(mizu.u64, taken, not_taken)

		end_label : _ = label()
		_ : _ = return(taken)
	}

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// `while`
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// `condition` is a block, spliced *inside* the loop, which is what makes
	// re-testing mean anything: a register is written once, so a test that is
	// to answer differently on the second iteration has to be recomputed, and
	// a block is the only thing that can be run twice. The block yields the
	// `u1`, and `execute` hands it back in the register the branch reads.
	//
	// The loop is rotated - the test sits at the bottom and is entered by a
	// jump - so the spec's zero iterations costs one jump on entry rather than
	// an inverted test and a second branch every time round. Which is also
	// what keeps the test out of the evaluator's hands: `branch_to` carries
	// `no_comptime` and `mizu.set_if_equal` deliberately does not, so an
	// inverted test over a condition block that yields a compile time known
	// value folds to a constant and the branch that was to end the loop is
	// never emitted. Nothing is left to invert here.
	//
	// Nothing here names a register. `if` needs its two arms merged because
	// they are two blocks that have to land in one register; a loop body is
	// spliced once, so `return(value)` is the whole of it.
	//
	// The spec's result type is `pointer(typeof(body))` - null when the loop
	// ran zero times - and this hands back the body's register instead.
	// Nothing here can build that pointer: there is no allocation on this path
	// and no null to store in it, so the caller cannot tell a loop that ran
	// zero times from one whose last iteration yielded zero.
	while_t : type = (condition: block, body: block) -> _
	_ : type = functions.always_inline(while_t)
	_ : type = functions.never_comptime(while_t)
	export while : while_t = {
		// Both addresses are found once, above the loop, rather than each time
		// round it: `find_label` searches the program, and the program does not
		// move.
		top_addr : _ = find_label(top_label)
		test_addr : _ = find_label(test_label)
		_ : _ = jump_to(test_addr)

		top_label : _ = label()
		value : _ = execute(body)

		test_label : _ = label()
		test : _ = execute(condition)
		_ : _ = branch_to(test, top_addr)

		_ : _ = return(value)
	}

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// `for`
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// `while` with the two extra splice points a counted loop wants: `init`
	// before the entry jump so it runs once, `step` between the body and the
	// test so it runs after every iteration. The rotation is what makes that
	// one ordering serve both - `step` lands ahead of the test without landing
	// ahead of the first one.
	//
	// What it does *not* do is scope the loop variable. The four blocks are
	// spliced at four separate points, each as a subblock of this body, so
	// R-Unqual gives none of them a view of another's declarations: whatever
	// `condition` and `step` share is declared in the caller and pinned, the
	// way a `while` body's own state is. A `for` that owns its variable needs a
	// block whose scope *encloses* the splices, which `execute` cannot express -
	// it splices where the call stands.
	for_t : type = (init: block, condition: block, step: block, body: block) -> _
	_ : type = functions.always_inline(for_t)
	_ : type = functions.never_comptime(for_t)
	export for : for_t = {
		top_addr : _ = find_label(top_label)
		test_addr : _ = find_label(test_label)

		_ : _ = execute(init)
		_ : _ = jump_to(test_addr)

		top_label : _ = label()
		value : _ = execute(body)
		_ : _ = execute(step)

		test_label : _ = label()
		test : _ = execute(condition)
		_ : _ = branch_to(test, top_addr)

		_ : _ = return(value)
	}

	// TODO: `defer` is not this shape, which is why it is still here. `if` and
	// `while` emit around a block the caller handed them; `defer` has to emit
	// its block at every exit point of the block that *called* it - after the
	// last statement, and ahead of each `return` and `yield` above it. A callee
	// cannot see any of that: `execute` splices where the call stands, and
	// there is no instruction that walks out to the caller's block and splices
	// once per terminator. The compiler-side pass that does it, or an
	// instruction that exposes the caller's block the way `block` exposes a
	// quoted one, is what this waits on.

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Mizu Core
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// `label` and `find_label` take a name in `standard.doir` and a 32 bit
	// immediate in Mizu, with only the first four characters significant.
	// `label_to_immediate` is the fold between them, which
	// `opt.mizu.materializeLabels` otherwise does compiler-side.
	export label : alias = mizu.label
	export find_label : alias = mizu.find_label
	export label_to_immediate : alias = mizu.doir.label_to_immediate

	export halt : alias = mizu.halt

	export debug_print : alias = mizu.debug_print
	export debug_print_binary : alias = mizu.debug_print_binary

	pointers : namespace = {
		// TODO: `load` and `store` dispatch on the size of `T` across the four
		// `stack_load_u*` / `stack_store_u*`. These are the 64 bit case.
		export load : alias = mizu.stack_load_u64
		export store : alias = mizu.stack_store_u64

		// TODO: Mizu has no instruction that dereferences a pointer. The
		// closest is `unsafe.copy_memory`, which reads its *output* register as
		// the destination pointer and so cannot be bound at all under the
		// calling convention `mizu.doir` emits. A `pointer_load_u{8,16,32,64}`
		// / `pointer_store_u{8,16,32,64}` family - the `stack_load_*` code with
		// `sp + offset` replaced by the register - is what these two want.

		// Registers are already immutable (SSA); memory reached through a
		// pointer is the only thing that is not.
		export immutable : alias = mizu.doir.type_comptime
	}

	stack : namespace = {
		export push : alias = mizu.stack_push
		export pop : alias = mizu.stack_pop
		export bottom : alias = mizu.pointer_to_stack_bottom
		export to_pointer : alias = mizu.pointer_to_stack

		// `push` moves the stack pointer *down* by its byte count, so the
		// region it just reserved begins at `to_pointer(0)` - which is what
		// makes `standard.doir`'s one-line `allocate(T)` these two
		// instructions.
		//
		// `size_bits` answers bits and the stack counts bytes. `shift_right` by
		// three is that divide, and the only comptime arithmetic the builtin
		// block offers - enough, because a type's size is compile time known
		// (C-Type), so the shift folds and the push takes an immediate.
		allocate_t : type = (T : type) -> T
		_ : type = functions.always_inline(allocate_t)
		// Emits; never runs. Its one argument is a type, so without this the
		// evaluator would fold the whole body and the stack would never move.
		_ : type = functions.never_comptime(allocate_t)
		export allocate : allocate_t = {
			// `x0` reads as zero on every Mizu machine, so the offset costs a
			// pin rather than an instruction - the trick `if` uses to compare.
			zero : mizu.u64 = 0
			_ : _ = compiler.assembler.pin_register(mizu.u64, zero, mizu.x0)

			%3 : index = 3
			bits : index = types.size_bits(T)
			bytes : index = compiler.shift_right(bits, %3)
			_ : _ = mizu.stack_push_immediate(bytes)

			ptr : byte_pointer = mizu.pointer_to_stack(zero)
			// TODO: At some point returning a byte_pointer from something expecting a T should be a type error!
			// But first we need to figure out how to reinterpret...
			_ : _ = return(ptr)
		}

		export allocate_bytes : alias = mizu.allocate
		export free_bytes : alias = mizu.free_allocated
	}

	export jump_relative : alias = mizu.jump_relative
	export jump_to : alias = mizu.jump_to

	export branch_relative : alias = mizu.branch_relative
	export branch_to : alias = mizu.branch_to

	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Arithmetic and comparison: the dispatch
	// -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// A Mizu register is 64 bits and nothing in it says how to read them, so
	// every operation from here to the end of the file is an emitter rather
	// than a renaming: it asks `T` which of the three kinds it is and emits the
	// matching instruction. `standard.doir` writes each of these as one
	// `(T: deduced type, ...)` function, which is what forces the question -
	// the alternative, an overload set per operation, waits on `add_overload`.
	//
	// The shape every one of them repeats:
	//
	//   - one quoted block per instruction, each yielding its result;
	//   - one comptime condition per block, read off `T`'s attribute id;
	//   - one `execute_if` per block, which splices the block into itself when
	//     the condition holds and deletes itself when it does not.
	//
	// Exactly one condition is true, so exactly one instruction is emitted and
	// the others never reach the machine. Everything above the `execute_if`s
	// is folded before the program exists: `kind` is a question about a type,
	// and the comparisons against it have two compile time known operands.
	//
	// Every surviving `execute_if` is named by a `return`. That is all `return`
	// does - it gives its argument the enclosing block's register (WF-Term) -
	// so the arms agree on where the result lands without any of them knowing
	// which one ran, exactly as `if`'s two arms do, and the deleted ones name a
	// register nothing emits. Inside an arm, `yield` does the same one level
	// down: it puts the instruction's result in the register of the
	// `execute_if` that spliced it.
	//
	// Two things this leans on, both argued at the schedule at the bottom of
	// the file. `opt.inlineFunctions` has to leave the chain
	// `kind` -> condition -> `execute_if` for the evaluator instead of
	// replacing each link with the bytes of the instruction that computes it -
	// in the declaration above as much as in the copies, since inlining the
	// chain here is inlining it in every copy made afterwards. And the arms are
	// quoted blocks declared inside a function body, so they have to travel
	// with a copy of that body - one arm per instantiation, each naming that
	// instantiation's arguments.
	//
	// The conditions are written in `mizu.set_if_*` rather than in `is_equal`
	// below, which would be a call to the very dispatch being defined.
	binary_t : type = (T: deduced type, a: T, b: T) -> T
	_ : type = functions.always_inline(binary_t)
	// Emits; never runs. For `if`'s reason: arithmetic on two compile time
	// known operands is the common case, and without this the evaluator folds
	// the call and the dispatch never emits anything.
	_ : type = functions.never_comptime(binary_t)
	// A comparison answers `u1` whatever it compared, so its result type is not
	// its argument type and it needs a signature of its own.
	compare_t : type = (T: deduced type, a: T, b: T) -> u1
	_ : type = functions.always_inline(compare_t)
	_ : type = functions.never_comptime(compare_t)
	predicate_t : type = (T: deduced type, v: T) -> u1
	_ : type = functions.always_inline(predicate_t)
	_ : type = functions.never_comptime(predicate_t)

	// The `Values` member, here for the reason given there. Same shape and same
	// tag as the arithmetic below, and `never_comptime` is not an optimization
	// here but the whole point: its argument is comptime by definition, so
	// C-Call would fold every call of it and the load would never be emitted.
	transition_t : type = (T: deduced type, v: T) -> T
	_ : type = functions.always_inline(transition_t)
	_ : type = functions.never_comptime(transition_t)

	// Two instructions, one encoding: `opt.mizu.materializeImmediates` splits
	// the value into the two halves a 32 bit immediate field needs. They differ
	// only in how they read it - an integer straight across, a float as the bit
	// pattern of the double - which is the same two-way question `add` asks, so
	// the signed and unsigned kinds share an arm here too.
	export transition_comptime_to_runtime : transition_t = {
		float_arm : block = {
			loaded : T = mizu.load_f64_immediate(T, v)
			_ : T = yield(loaded)
		}
		integer_arm : block = {
			loaded : T = mizu.load_u64_immediate(T, v)
			_ : T = yield(loaded)
		}
		kind : index = attributes.get_id_of_type(T)
		floating : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, float_kind)
		integral : u1 = mizu.set_if_not_equal(kind, float_kind)
		float_result : _ = execute_if(float_arm, floating)
		integer_result : _ = execute_if(integer_arm, integral)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, float_result, integer_result)
		_ : _ = return(float_result)
		_ : _ = return(integer_result) // TODO: Why does emission hinge on a return? Can it hinge on a future use instead?
	}

	// V-Copy. `transition_comptime_to_runtime`'s sibling, and it shares that
	// one's type: the other puts a compile time known value into its register,
	// this one puts a value into a *different* register.
	//
	// `v + 0`, with the zero coming from `x0`, which reads as zero on every
	// Mizu machine and so costs no instruction of its own - the trick `if` uses
	// to name it. It was `mizu.convert_to_u64`, which is the same move (its
	// body is `registers[out] = registers[a]` and converts nothing) under a
	// foldable type, and that is the whole difference: a copy whose source is
	// compile time known - which an argument is as often as not - folded to its
	// own operand and emitted nothing, leaving the register it was to write
	// unwritten. `never_comptime` here says it must not.
	//
	// The flag sits on this type rather than on `mizu.add`'s because folding
	// arithmetic is exactly what makes the dispatches work; `v` reaching
	// `mizu.add` as a *parameter* is what keeps the inner call unfoldable.
	//
	// `mizu.add` rather than `add` below: integer addition is bit exact for any
	// 64 bit pattern, and the dispatch would add `0.0` to an `f64`, which is not
	// the same thing for a signed zero. A copy moves bits, not numbers.
	export copy : transition_t = {
		zero : mizu.u64 = 0
		_ : _ = compiler.assembler.pin_register(mizu.u64, zero, mizu.x0)
		moved : _ = mizu.add(v, zero)
		_ : _ = return(moved)
	}

	// Two's complement is what makes this a two-way question rather than a
	// three-way one: one instruction adds signed and unsigned alike, and the
	// same goes for `subtract` and `multiply`. `divide`, `modulus` and the
	// ordering comparisons are where signedness starts to cost an instruction.
	export add : binary_t = {
		float_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.add_f64(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		integer_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.add(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		kind : index = attributes.get_id_of_type(T)
		floating : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, float_kind)
		integral : u1 = mizu.set_if_not_equal(kind, float_kind)
		float_result : _ = execute_if(float_arm, floating)
		integer_result : _ = execute_if(integer_arm, integral)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, float_result, integer_result)
		_ : _ = return(float_result)
		_ : _ = return(integer_result) // TODO: Why does emission hinge on a return? Can it hinge on a future use instead?
	}

	export subtract : binary_t = {
		float_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.subtract_f64(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		integer_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.subtract(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		kind : index = attributes.get_id_of_type(T)
		floating : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, float_kind)
		integral : u1 = mizu.set_if_not_equal(kind, float_kind)
		float_result : _ = execute_if(float_arm, floating)
		integer_result : _ = execute_if(integer_arm, integral)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, float_result, integer_result)
		_ : _ = return(float_result)
		_ : _ = return(integer_result) // TODO: Why does emission hinge on a return? Can it hinge on a future use instead?
	}

	export multiply : binary_t = {
		float_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.multiply_f64(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		integer_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.multiply(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		kind : index = attributes.get_id_of_type(T)
		floating : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, float_kind)
		integral : u1 = mizu.set_if_not_equal(kind, float_kind)
		float_result : _ = execute_if(float_arm, floating)
		integer_result : _ = execute_if(integer_arm, integral)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, float_result, integer_result)
		_ : _ = return(float_result)
		_ : _ = return(integer_result) // TODO: Why does emission hinge on a return? Can it hinge on a future use instead?
	}

	// Two arms, not three, though this is where signedness starts to matter:
	// Mizu has `divide` and `divide_f64` and nothing between them - no
	// `divide_signed`, unlike the shifts and the ordering comparisons - so a
	// signed `T` gets the unsigned instruction, which is wrong for every
	// negative operand. Naming the hole is all a dispatch can do about it; see
	// the note under `modulus`.
	export divide : binary_t = {
		float_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.divide_f64(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		integer_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.divide(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		kind : index = attributes.get_id_of_type(T)
		floating : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, float_kind)
		integral : u1 = mizu.set_if_not_equal(kind, float_kind)
		float_result : _ = execute_if(float_arm, floating)
		integer_result : _ = execute_if(integer_arm, integral)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, float_result, integer_result)
		_ : _ = return(float_result)
		_ : _ = return(integer_result) // TODO: Why does emission hinge on a return? Can it hinge on a future use instead?
	}

	// Not a dispatch, and this is the rule the ones above follow: an operation
	// dispatches exactly where Mizu has more than one instruction to dispatch
	// *to*. `modulus` has one - integer, unsigned - and no float or signed
	// counterpart, so every arm this could grow would either emit the same
	// instruction under a different condition or emit nothing at all, and an
	// arm that emits nothing leaves the result register unwritten. A rename
	// that is wrong for two of the three kinds is at least visibly a rename.
	//
	// TODO: `modulus_f64`, `divide_signed` and `modulus_signed` are what this
	// and `divide`'s integer arm are waiting on. They are Mizu's to add.
	export modulus : alias = mizu.modulus

	// Four comparisons, not six: the set is closed under swapping operands, so
	// `a > b` is `is_less(b, a)` and `a <= b` is `is_greater_equal(b, a)`.
	//
	// Equality is two-way - the bit patterns compare the same signed or
	// unsigned, and only the float instruction differs, because it has NaN to
	// account for. Ordering is three-way.
	export is_equal : compare_t = {
		float_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.set_if_equal_f64(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		integer_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.set_if_equal(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		kind : index = attributes.get_id_of_type(T)
		floating : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, float_kind)
		integral : u1 = mizu.set_if_not_equal(kind, float_kind)
		float_result : _ = execute_if(float_arm, floating)
		integer_result : _ = execute_if(integer_arm, integral)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, float_result, integer_result)
		_ : _ = return(float_result)
		_ : _ = return(integer_result) // TODO: Why does emission hinge on a return? Can it hinge on a future use instead?
	}

	export is_not_equal : compare_t = {
		float_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.set_if_not_equal_f64(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		integer_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.set_if_not_equal(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		kind : index = attributes.get_id_of_type(T)
		floating : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, float_kind)
		integral : u1 = mizu.set_if_not_equal(kind, float_kind)
		float_result : _ = execute_if(float_arm, floating)
		integer_result : _ = execute_if(integer_arm, integral)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, float_result, integer_result)
		_ : _ = return(float_result)
		_ : _ = return(integer_result) // TODO: Why does emission hinge on a return? Can it hinge on a future use instead?
	}

	export is_less : compare_t = {
		float_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.set_if_less_f64(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		signed_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.set_if_less_signed(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		unsigned_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.set_if_less(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		kind : index = attributes.get_id_of_type(T)
		floating : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, float_kind)
		signed : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, signed_kind)
		unsigned : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, unsigned_kind)
		float_result : _ = execute_if(float_arm, floating)
		signed_result : _ = execute_if(signed_arm, signed)
		unsigned_result : _ = execute_if(unsigned_arm, unsigned)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, float_result, signed_result)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, signed_result, unsigned_result)
		_ : _ = return(float_result)
		_ : _ = return(signed_result)
		_ : _ = return(unsigned_result)
	}

	export is_greater_equal : compare_t = {
		float_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.set_if_greater_equal_f64(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		signed_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.set_if_greater_equal_signed(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		unsigned_arm : block = {
			v : _ = mizu.set_if_greater_equal(a, b)
			_ : _ = yield(v)
		}
		kind : index = attributes.get_id_of_type(T)
		floating : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, float_kind)
		signed : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, signed_kind)
		unsigned : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, unsigned_kind)
		float_result : _ = execute_if(float_arm, floating)
		signed_result : _ = execute_if(signed_arm, signed)
		unsigned_result : _ = execute_if(unsigned_arm, unsigned)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, float_result, signed_result)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, signed_result, unsigned_result)
		_ : _ = return(float_result)
		_ : _ = return(signed_result)
		_ : _ = return(unsigned_result)
	}

	// The signed comparisons `standard.doir` does not declare, kept because
	// they are what `is_less`'s signed arm emits and a program that knows its
	// operands are signed should not have to tag a type to say so.
	export is_less_signed : alias = mizu.set_if_less_signed
	export is_greater_equal_signed : alias = mizu.set_if_greater_equal_signed

	// Float only, and aliases for `modulus`'s reason: there is no integer
	// `max`, `min` or `sqrt` in Mizu's core to dispatch to. `max` and `min` are
	// a comparison and a branch away - which this file can now write, but not
	// without a label, and a label inside a body inlined at two call sites is
	// what the schedule's third note is about. Integer `sqrt` is not
	// synthesizable at all.
	export max : alias = mizu.max_f64
	export min : alias = mizu.min_f64
	export sqrt : alias = mizu.sqrt_f64

	export shift_left : alias = mizu.shift_left
	export shift_right : alias = mizu.shift_right_logical
	export shift_right_arithmetic : alias = mizu.shift_right_arithmetic
	export bitwise_xor : alias = mizu.bitwise_xor
	export bitwise_or : alias = mizu.bitwise_or
	export bitwise_and : alias = mizu.bitwise_and
	// No bitwise_not: it is bitwise_xor against an all-ones register.

	// The sign predicates dispatch; `is_infinity` and `is_nan` cannot, because
	// only a float has either, so for an integer the honest answer is a
	// constant rather than an instruction - and there is no way to *return a
	// constant* here. An arm has to yield a register, and nothing loads one
	// without an instruction to load it with.
	export is_negative : predicate_t = {
		float_arm : block = {
			r : _ = mizu.set_if_negative_f64(v)
			_ : _ = yield(r)
		}
		// Two's complement: negative is the top bit, which is what
		// `set_if_less_signed` against `x0` reads. `mizu.x0` is zero on every
		// Mizu machine, so this is one instruction and no constant.
		signed_arm : block = {
			zero : mizu.u64 = 0
			_ : _ = compiler.assembler.pin_register(mizu.u64, zero, mizu.x0)
			r : _ = mizu.set_if_less_signed(v, zero)
			_ : _ = yield(r)
		}
		// An unsigned value is never negative, and `set_if_less` against zero
		// is how that constant gets into a register.
		unsigned_arm : block = {
			zero : mizu.u64 = 0
			_ : _ = compiler.assembler.pin_register(mizu.u64, zero, mizu.x0)
			r : _ = mizu.set_if_less(v, zero)
			_ : _ = yield(r)
		}
		kind : index = attributes.get_id_of_type(T)
		floating : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, float_kind)
		signed : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, signed_kind)
		unsigned : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, unsigned_kind)
		float_result : _ = execute_if(float_arm, floating)
		signed_result : _ = execute_if(signed_arm, signed)
		unsigned_result : _ = execute_if(unsigned_arm, unsigned)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, float_result, signed_result)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, signed_result, unsigned_result)
		_ : _ = return(float_result)
		_ : _ = return(signed_result)
		_ : _ = return(unsigned_result)
	}

	export is_positive : predicate_t = {
		float_arm : block = {
			r : _ = mizu.set_if_positive_f64(v)
			_ : _ = yield(r)
		}
		signed_arm : block = {
			zero : mizu.u64 = 0
			_ : _ = compiler.assembler.pin_register(mizu.u64, zero, mizu.x0)
			r : _ = mizu.set_if_greater_equal_signed(v, zero)
			_ : _ = yield(r)
		}
		unsigned_arm : block = {
			zero : mizu.u64 = 0
			_ : _ = compiler.assembler.pin_register(mizu.u64, zero, mizu.x0)
			r : _ = mizu.set_if_greater_equal(v, zero)
			_ : _ = yield(r)
		}
		kind : index = attributes.get_id_of_type(T)
		floating : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, float_kind)
		signed : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, signed_kind)
		unsigned : u1 = mizu.set_if_equal(kind, unsigned_kind)
		float_result : _ = execute_if(float_arm, floating)
		signed_result : _ = execute_if(signed_arm, signed)
		unsigned_result : _ = execute_if(unsigned_arm, unsigned)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, float_result, signed_result)
		_ : T = unsafe.alias_temporaries(T, signed_result, unsigned_result)
		_ : _ = return(float_result)
		_ : _ = return(signed_result)
		_ : _ = return(unsigned_result)
	}

	// Float only. An integer is neither, and saying so takes a constant this
	// file has no way to produce - see the note above `is_negative`.
	export is_infinity : alias = mizu.set_if_infinity_f64
	export is_nan : alias = mizu.set_if_nan_f64

	// The float instructions, reachable without going through the dispatch -
	// and the conversions, which nothing above can reach at all. `standard.doir`
	// never named any of them because it assumed one overload set per
	// operation.
	//
	// Named `float` rather than `f64`, which is what it was: `f64` is now a
	// type at the root, and by R-Unqual a bare `f64` anywhere inside `std`
	// would find this namespace instead and the type would be unspellable from
	// the whole file.
	float : namespace = {
		export add : alias = mizu.add_f64
		export subtract : alias = mizu.subtract_f64
		export multiply : alias = mizu.multiply_f64
		export divide : alias = mizu.divide_f64
		export is_equal : alias = mizu.set_if_equal_f64
		export is_not_equal : alias = mizu.set_if_not_equal_f64
		export is_less : alias = mizu.set_if_less_f64
		export is_greater_equal : alias = mizu.set_if_greater_equal_f64
		export from_unsigned : alias = mizu.convert_to_f64
		export from_signed : alias = mizu.convert_signed_to_f64
		export to_unsigned : alias = mizu.convert_from_f64
		export to_signed : alias = mizu.convert_signed_from_f64
	}
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The schedule
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `mizu.doir`'s own schedule is left exactly as it is: it lowers a program
// written in Mizu instructions, and that is all it has to do. This one is the
// same list with the four changes the standard interface needs, and it is
// nominated from here rather than there because they are this layer's
// requirements, not the backend's. Nomination is outermost-then-bottom-most, so
// including this file after `mizu.doir` is what makes it win.
//
//   1. `comptimeEvaluateVisitor` after `inlineFunctions`. A meta-level call
//      inside a function body - `std.execute(t)` on a `block` parameter - reads
//      a value that only a call site can supply, so it cannot run until
//      inlining has substituted one in. Every control flow construct below is
//      built this way, which is why `mizu.doir` does not need the pass and this
//      file cannot do without it.
//   2. Registers again afterwards, because a body copied into a call site is
//      new entities and a copy carries no `AssignedRegister`.
//   3. `materializeLabels` after inlining rather than before. A label id is
//      what `find_label` searches for, a copy does not carry one, and two
//      inlinings of one `if` must not answer to the same label.
//   4. `stripFreestandingBlocks` once more, after the evaluation. The dispatch
//      arms are quoted blocks declared inside a function body, which
//      `canon.stripFreestandingBlocks` leaves where they are so that a copy of
//      the body brings them along; once a call site has been inlined and its
//      dispatch resolved, the copies are ordinary module-scope quoted blocks
//      with nothing left to splice them.
schedule : compiler.byte_pointer = """
	sequential(
		depthFirst(nameReuse),
		depthFirst(functionArity),
		breadthFirst(foldBaseTypes),
		depthFirst(typeCheck),
		depthFirst(computeTypeProperties),

		applyGlobally(sequential(
			sort,
			depthFirst(pinRegisters),
			breadthFirst(computeAllocatorInputs),
			assignTemporaries,
			depthFirst(pinRegisters)
		)),

		breadthFirst(computeCompilerNamespace!false),
		depthFirst(materializeImmediates),
		sorted(monomorphizeFunctions, false),
		breadthFirst(inlineFunctions),

		// One walk does a whole dispatch: it is depth first, so it reaches
		// `kind` before the comparison that reads it and the comparison before
		// the `execute_if` that reads *that*.
		depthFirst(comptimeEvaluateVisitor),
		// The dispatch arms, now that the dispatch has chosen between them.
		// `canon.stripFreestandingBlocks` leaves a quoted block declared inside
		// a function body alone, because a copy of that body has to bring its
		// blocks with it - `std.add`'s arms name its parameters. The copies it
		// brought are at module scope and spent by the time the line above is
		// done.
		//
		// Which is also why no arm here holds a comptime call: an arm is walked
		// whether or not it was chosen, so a fold with an effect - a
		// `diagnostics.error` on the kind that has no instruction - fires from
		// the arm that was about to be deleted. Every arm in this file emits
		// and does nothing else.
		breadthFirst(stripFreestandingBlocks),

		// 5. The functions inlining did not consume, which have to be emitted
		//    rather than substituted. Two passes because a call can precede
		//    its callee: the first gives each surviving function an entry and
		//    an exit label and records the pair on it, the second rewrites
		//    calls and returns into jumps to those labels. Here rather than
		//    earlier because "still standing" is only true once inlining and
		//    the comptime dispatch above have taken out everything they were
		//    going to.
		// The bodies must not sit in the instruction stream they are called
		// from, or the top level code walks into the first one instead of
		// reaching the end. `sort` after it because reordering a block leaves
		// its children no longer in the post-order the ids carry.
		sinkDeclarations,
		sort,
		depthFirst(claimFunctionLabels),
		depthFirst(lowerFunctionCalls),
		// After `lowerFunctionCalls`, which finds a `return`'s owner by walking
		// up to the enclosing function, and before the register round, which is
		// where a lifted body's instructions get allocated alongside the top
		// level code rather than not at all.
		liftFunctionBodies,
		// Again, for the body that just came out. `stripFreestandingBlocks`
		// leaves a quoted block inside a function body alone and says so on
		// the grounds that nothing emits one; a lifted body is emitted, so its
		// spent dispatch arms - which are at module scope now like any other -
		// have to go the way the ones at the top level went above.
		breadthFirst(stripFreestandingBlocks),
		// The bodies those two spliced in - `impl.call`, `impl.prologue` and
		// the rest - are `always_inline`, and the register round below has to
		// see the instructions inside them rather than the calls to them.
		breadthFirst(inlineFunctions),

		// `impl.call`'s marshalling is comptime work - a recursive unroll over
		// the call's arguments, carrying `meta.argument_count`, `execute_if`
		// and the `a0 + i` that names each slot - and it arrives *after* the
		// evaluation above, because `lowerFunctionCalls` is what put it there.
		// So the evaluator runs again.
		//
		// To a fixed point, which the unroll is the reason for: each turn
		// splices the next call to `pass_arguments`, and a depth first walk
		// has already gone past the place it appears. One walk unrolls one
		// argument.
		// `bubbleComptime` first, and it is not optional: C-Call is what marks
		// a call compile time known, it runs in `canonicalizeSchedule` long
		// before any of this, and nothing has marked the calls
		// `lowerFunctionCalls` just spliced. Without it the evaluator finds no
		// comptime call to evaluate and the unroll stays a declaration.
		fixedPoint(sequential(
			depthFirst(bubbleComptime),
			depthFirst(comptimeEvaluateVisitor)
		)),
		// The arms the unroll spent, and the `copy` bodies it spliced - the
		// same pair of chores the evaluation above owes, for the same reasons.
		breadthFirst(stripFreestandingBlocks),
		breadthFirst(inlineFunctions),

		applyGlobally(sequential(
			depthFirst(pinRegisters),
			breadthFirst(computeAllocatorInputs),
			assignTemporaries,
			depthFirst(pinRegisters)
		)),
		// 6. `materializeImmediates` a second time, for the copies inlining
		//    made. `std.transition_comptime_to_runtime` wraps the immediate
		//    loads, so inside its declaration the value is a parameter and the
		//    pass leaves the call alone; the constant only exists in the copy a
		//    call site got. Here rather than straight after the inlining
		//    because it reads the destination's register off the entity and a
		//    copy carries none until the round above.
		depthFirst(materializeImmediates),
		depthFirst(materializeLabels),
		// `materializeLabels` does not emit a whole label instruction: it emits
		// the operand half and an `inline mizu.label_op()` for the opcode's
		// eight bytes, since the instruction id is `mizu.doir`'s to know and not
		// the compiler's. So it owes an inliner afterwards. `mizu.doir` pays
		// that by running it *before* `inlineFunctions`; moving it after, as
		// point 3 above does, means paying it here instead - without this line
		// each label emits eight bytes rather than sixteen and every
		// instruction after it decodes against the wrong boundary.
		breadthFirst(inlineFunctions),
		breadthFirst(computeCompilerNamespace!false),
		breadthFirst(computeCompilerNamespace!true)
	)
"""
_ : compiler.pointer_sized = compiler.override_fallback_schedule(schedule)
